  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $x\u226e\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $\u00acP(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "In plain English, our predicate $P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is not smaller than $\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nThe symbol \"$\u00ac$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is not smaller than $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nIn other words, $x$ *is* *smaller than* $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, $\u00acP(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x<\\frac{1}{2}$",
